      "text": "We start with the equation given in the problem:\n\\[ |n + 6| = 2 - n \\]\n\nThe absolute value function \\( |x| \\) can be split into two cases: one where \\( x \\geq 0 \\) and one where \\( x < 0 \\). Applying this to our equation, we need to consider two cases: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( n + 6 < 0 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). The equation becomes:\n\\[ n + 6 = 2 - n \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ n + n + 6 = 2 \\]\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\n\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n = 2 - 6 \\]\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\n\nThen, we divide both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\n\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[ -2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0 \\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n**Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6 \\). The equation becomes:\n\\[ -n - 6 = 2 - n \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ -6 = 2 \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ -6 = 2 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution for this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-2} \\]",
