    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression \\( |n + 6| \\) represents the distance of \\( n + 6 \\) from 0 on the number line. This distance can be either non-negative or zero, depending on whether \\( n + 6 \\) is non-negative or negative.\n\nThe equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) implies that \\( n + 6 \\) is either equal to \\( 2 - n \\) or \\( -(2 - n) \\). Therefore, we have two cases to consider:\n\n**Case 1:** \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\nTo solve this equation, we first add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ n + 6 + n = 2 - n + n \\]\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6 \\]\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\nThen, we divide both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\nWe need to check if \\( n = -2 \\) satisfies the original equation. Substituting \\( n = -2 \\) into the original equation, we get:\n\\[ | -2 + 6 | = 2 - (-2) \\]\n\\[ | 4 | = 2 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 4 = 4 \\]\nSince both sides of the equation are equal, \\( n = -2 \\) is a solution.\n\n**Case 2:** \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\nTo solve this equation, we first simplify the right-hand side:\n\\[ n + 6 = -2 + n \\]\nNext, we subtract \\( n \\) from both sides:\n\\[ n + 6 - n = -2 + n - n \\]\n\\[ 6 = -2 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution for \\( n \\) in this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
